<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Transportation – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Transportation</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Benefits of Public Transportation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write instructions for a simple task. Interactive: Create responses to class survey questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple instructions for taking transportation to a specific location (e.g., "To travel from Changuniolo to Panama City, first go to the bus terminal and purchase your ticket. Then, board the bus heading to Albrook Terminal. The trip takes about ten hours, so bring snacks and water. Once you arrive, you can take a taxi or the metro to your final destination.")
            Can create responses to survey questions about transportation preferences (e.g., "Answer with your preference: bus or car, and why.")
            Can write simple instructions about road safety rules (e.g., Some road safety rules are: wear your seatbelt in the car, do not cross the street when the red light is on, and walk on the left side of the sidewalk.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students participate in a "Think-Pair-Share" activity. The teacher asks students to think about different types of public transportation they know (bus, taxi, metro) and one benefit of each. Students individually jot down their ideas. Then, they pair up to share and compare their notes. Finally, the teacher asks a few pairs to share their ideas with the class. For example, "The bus is more convenient and cheaper than a taxi for long distances."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes two example sentences on the board:
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"To get to the school, you should take the Route 1 bus. It’s faster than walking."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I prefer riding my bicycle because it is the cheapest way to travel and avoids traffic."</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher reads the sentences aloud, focusing on the stress in "transportation" and "convenience," and distinguishing between /æ/ in "passenger" and /ɑː/ in "port." The teacher highlights the use of "should" for advice and "faster/cheapest" for comparison, explaining that today's focus is on writing similar instructions and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we writing about general ideas or specific transportation options?" (Expected: Specific options)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we just list words, or do we write complete sentences?" (Expected: Complete sentences)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short texts of instructions for taking public transportation and responses to survey questions about transportation preferences. The teacher provides handouts or projects two examples on the board:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example 1 (Instructions):</b> "To travel from Changuniolo to Panama City, first you have to go to the bus terminal and purchase your ticket. Then, board the bus heading to Albrook Terminal. The journey takes about ten hours, so you should bring snacks and water. Once you arrive, you can take a taxi or the metro to your final destination. Always remember to wear your seatbelt for safety."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example 2 (Survey Responses):</b>
                        <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Survey Question: Do you prefer public or private transportation for your daily commute, and why?</i></p>
                        <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Student A:</i> "I prefer public transportation, especially the metro, because it is more efficient and usually faster than driving a car in rush hour traffic. It's also a safer way to commute, and the fare is affordable."</p>
                        <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Student B:</i> "I prefer private transportation like a car because it's more convenient, and I don't have to follow a bus schedule. However, it can be expensive, and I have to avoid heavy traffic on the highway."</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads the examples aloud, modeling stress on "schedule," "passenger," and "vehicle," and practicing minimal pairs like /æ/ in "tap" versus /eɪ/ in "tape."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Writing: Can write simple instructions for taking transportation to a specific location; Can create responses to survey questions about transportation preferences; Can write simple instructions about road safety rules.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the examples silently.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The instructions suggest bringing snacks for a short trip." (False) "Student A thinks public transportation is less efficient." (False)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match phrases from the examples to their purpose (e.g., "first...then" to 'sequence', "safer way" to 'benefit').</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using modal verbs for advice and obligation (e.g., "You should take the bus." "You have to stop at red lights."), comparatives and superlatives (e.g., "The train is faster than the bus." "The plane is the fastest."), and phrasal verbs related to transportation (e.g., "get on," "get off," "tap in," "hop on"). They will integrate vocabulary: transportation, benefits, rules, road, safety, convenience, traffic, fare, passenger, seatbelt, traffic light, sidewalk, travel, follow, suggest, compare, prefer, choose, commute, wear, board, arrive, avoid.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill & Sentence Rewriting):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete sentences with appropriate modal verbs (should/have to) or comparative/superlative adjectives, and target vocabulary. E.g., "When you are a passenger, you _______ wear your seatbelt." "The train is _______ (fast) than the bus." "To travel, you need to _______ your card to _______ the metro."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students rewrite sentences to include a phrasal verb. E.g., "Enter the bus." (Rewrite: "Get on the bus.") "Exit the train." (Rewrite: "Get off the train.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled (Guided Sentence Construction):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use sentence frames to write short instructions for simple tasks related to road safety. E.g., "To cross the street safely, you _______ look both ways. You _______ cross when the light is red."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students respond to prompts using comparatives/superlatives and reasons. E.g., "Which transportation is _______ (safe), a car or a bus? Why?" (Response: "A bus is _______ (safe) than a car for long journeys because it has fewer accidents.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Edit):</b> Students exchange their written sentences from the previous activities with a partner. They read each other's sentences aloud to identify any pronunciation issues with multisyllabic words or long/short vowels, and then provide constructive feedback focusing on grammar accuracy (modals, comparatives), correct vocabulary use, and clarity of instructions. The teacher provides a simple checklist (e.g., "Did my partner use 'should' or 'have to' correctly?", "Are all key vocabulary words spelled right?", "Are the sentences easy to understand?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Transportation".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work individually or in pairs to produce two short written paragraphs based on prompts related to the theme "The Benefits of Public Transportation."</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task A (Instructions):</b> Write a paragraph giving instructions for taking a specific public transportation route in your city/town (e.g., from your home to a shopping center or a local park), including at least two road safety rules or travel tips. Use sequence words (first, then, next, finally) and modal verbs (should, have to).
                    <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Example Prompt:</i> "Imagine you need to take the bus from your school to a famous landmark in your city. Write clear instructions, and mention one safety rule passengers should follow."</p>
                    <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Student example:</i> "To travel from [Your School Name] to [Famous Landmark], first you should walk to the nearest bus stop for Route 5. Then, you have to tap in with your card when you get on the bus. The journey takes about 20 minutes, so you can enjoy the view. Finally, get off at the [Landmark] stop. Remember, passengers should always wear their seatbelts if available for safety."</p></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task B (Survey Response):</b> Write a paragraph responding to the following survey question: "Which mode of transportation do you prefer for long-distance travel (e.g., plane, bus, train, car) and why? Compare it to at least one other option, highlighting its benefits." Incorporate comparatives/superlatives and vocabulary related to benefits, convenience, and efficiency.
                    <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Example Prompt:</i> "Write about your preferred mode for long journeys, explaining why it's better than another option."</p>
                    <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; text-align: justify;"><i>Student example:</i> "For long-distance travel, I prefer the train. It is more comfortable and often more convenient than a bus because you can walk around. Also, the train is faster than a car, especially when there is heavy traffic on the highway. I think it's one of the safest ways to travel, and you don't have to drive."</p></li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering guidance, checking for correct use of grammar and vocabulary, and encouraging students to produce coherent and clear written pieces.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their two written paragraphs from Stage 4. Partners also use a simple checklist to assess each other's paragraphs before final submission.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and logical sequence of instructions (Task A).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of modal verbs (should, have to) for advice/obligation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of comparatives/superlatives for comparisons (Task B).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary and phrasal verbs related to transportation (e.g., 'tap in', 'get on', 'get off').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall coherence and sentence structure suitable for A2.4 level.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of road safety rules or travel tips (Task A).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two reflective prompts before leaving the class.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now writing instructions for taking transportation?" (Scale of 1-5, with 5 being very confident)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar point you used in your writing today, and how did it help you express your ideas?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your instructions or survey responses today? How did you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the instructions you wrote today be useful for someone new to your city/town?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the writing prompts, clear understanding of modal verbs)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with writing coherent paragraphs or applying the grammar points (e.g., providing more sentence starters, focused mini-lessons on specific vocabulary)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the lesson effectively address the writing objectives for A2.4 level?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "In our next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration), we will consolidate all the skills we've practiced (listening, reading, speaking, and writing) to complete a project about improving public transportation in our community. We will summarize information from different sources and present our ideas to propose solutions for safer and more efficient travel."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>